Z||000000||TylerB11||08-14-2001||12:38 PM||||I have a 85GT, 4spd. The shifter was so sloppy, and I couldn't get reverse sometimes. SO i looked at the trans and the selector bushings were shot. so i got the Rodney Dickman ones and popped em in. Better, but no reverse. Adjusted the selector arm, no reverse. I took off the shifter arm and guess what? THe bracket that's tack welded to the sleeve was no longer welded. Thank god my dad has been aching to use his MiG welder..<P>Anyway, if anyone is having problems with reverse, make sure to check that weld too..||||reg||1
Z||000001||polomeno||08-14-2001||04:24 PM||||Yes!! they are pieces of ****  , mine broke today right at the weld !! just like 2 hours ago.. was at a red light on way to work and went to go into 1st and the damn thing snapped right off in my hand... dunno how i'm gettin home.. damn shifters!!! and I dont have a welder, was thinkin about trying some JD weld on it.. <P>John||||reg||1
Z||000002||TylerB11||08-14-2001||04:26 PM||||No, I meant the other end, the shifter arm on the trans(not the selector arm, the shifter).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||85ZainoGT||08-14-2001||11:20 PM||||I have that same damn problem, everytime I put it in reverse it grinds(yes I have the clutch in) it jus does it about 90% of the time, and UGH... and I cant really shift to fast, is there anything I can do?||||reg||1
Z||000004||TylerB11||08-15-2001||10:26 AM||||THis is for the muncie 4 speed..<P>Look where the cables for the shifter come to the trans (to the left of the engine) You'll see two levers. the flat, longer one is the shifter arm (it does the 1-N-2 3-N-4 R-N shifting) and other one is the selector arm (it does the left and right shifting) The way it works is:<BR>there is a bar coming out of the trans. by twisting it (shifter arm) it does up and down, by pulling it in and out, it does left and right shifting.<P>There are two points there that will give you really sloppy shifting.<P>there is a pin that goes through the selector arm and trans. it has a carter key on the end, take the key off, and tap out the pin (it gets stuck on the webbing of the trans, so you have to tap it with a pair of pliers or something). Use a 9/16 wrench to take the selector cable off the arm. Make sure the shifter arm is in the middle (neutral). remove the selector bracket. It should have two bushings in it (plastic). Replace them with rodney dickmans brass bushings, much better. <P>Now on the end of the shifter, you will see a nut on a rectangular bolt (threads on the two ends, thats it)  take the nut and washer off. take the 9/16 bot holding the shifter cable onto the arm too. take off the bracket. it should be a bracket with a rectangular hole, and a sleeve welded to it. in that sleeve is a slot where the selector arm moves the shifter in and out. make sure the sleeve is securely on the bracket and that the slot isn't all worn out. <BR>put it back together, and you should be good to go. there is some adjustment where the cables meet the brackets (slotted holes). adjust accordingly.||||reg||1
